<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.linuxfs.plist

  Mounts the filesystems the Linux ABI needs, at boot.

  WHY org.nextbsd.* AND NOT com.apple.*

  Same reasoning as org.nextbsd.wland: Apple ships no Linux ABI and therefore no
  daemon this models, so a com.apple.* label would be fabricated. Nothing
  Apple-derived looks this label up either.

  WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL

  On stock FreeBSD rc.d/linux does this. NextBSD has launchd as PID 1 and no
  rc.d, and /etc/fstab is inert here because nothing runs `mount -a` — so the
  Linux ABI compiled in by nextbsd-kernel#61 is present but unusable without it:

    * empty /proc          -> Node/Bun abort at startup
    * missing /dev/fd/N    -> Bun resolves its cwd via open(cwd, O_PATH) then
                              readlink("/dev/fd/N"); devfs supplies only 0,1,2,
                              so claude-code dies with
                              "Can't access working directory ...: does not exist"
                              (nextbsd-kernel#65 compiles in fdescfs; this mounts it)

  RunAtLoad with KeepAlive FALSE: this is a one-shot, not a daemon. KeepAlive
  true would respawn it in a tight loop forever once the mounts are done.

  No launchd ordering exists in this port (no StartAfter/Requires, and the plist
  directory scan is not sorted), so the script must tolerate running before a
  Linux userland is installed — it exits 0 when /compat/linux is absent, and is
  idempotent so it can simply be kickstarted again afterwards:

      launchctl kickstart -k system/org.nextbsd.linuxfs
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.linuxfs</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/libexec/nextbsd-linuxfs</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/linuxfs.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/linuxfs.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
